JIRA ID : AIHPBLAS-4994

Stack, in merge order: #12876, **#12960 (this PR)**, #13005, #13013,
#13015, #13021, #13022, #13025, #13031. The diff here is only the
placement harness; the base moves to `develop` when #12876 merges.

## Summary

Some address bugs don't depend on how big the problem is. They depend on
where the buffer sits. If an operand straddles a 4 GiB boundary, a
kernel that drops the carry reads or writes exactly 4 GiB away. With
`hipMalloc` that happens rarely and at random, so a test can pass for
months and then fail once with no way to reproduce it. fast_check's
poison and sentinel can't see an access that far outside the buffer, and
on some GPUs that stray access faults hard enough to need a reset.

This PR adds controlled placement. A `placement` argument maps a chosen
operand (A, B, C, D, bias, the scale vector or the workspace) across a 4
GiB boundary using HIP virtual memory. It also maps poison windows
exactly 4 GiB above and below. A bad read gets poison, so fast_check
reports a wrong answer instead of the GPU faulting. A bad write lands in
the poison, where a scan finds it. So a rare, random crash becomes a
deterministic, safe test.

A self-test simulates a dropped carry and checks that it's caught. Two
deliberate mutations (checking only one window, and never filling the
poison) each make that self-test fail. The motivating defect,
ROCM-32046, is in gfx1250 MXFP8 Stream-K kernels, which no GPU here can
run; MX operands get placement in #13031, and the cases here check f32
and bf16 kernels on every GPU CI runs.

**CI time added** (one MI300X / one MI350X, ROCm 10.2, sum of per-test
times): about 1 s / under 1 s in pre_checkin (12 cases), and about 2 min
/ 40 s in nightly (14 every-solution cases).

## Motivation

Some hipBLASLt address defects depend on where a buffer sits in memory
rather than on the problem size. ROCM-32046 drops the carry out of the
low 32 bits of an address in two MXFP8 Stream-K solutions on gfx1250, so
it goes wrong only when an operand's base crosses a 4 GiB boundary. With
`hipMalloc` that crossing is rare and random, so a test can pass for
months and then fail once with no reproduction. The wrong access lands
exactly 4 GiB away, outside every allocation. fast_check's padding
poison and D sentinel cannot see it, and on gfx1250 the resulting page
fault can leave the GPU unusable until it is reset (ROCM-32049).

## Technical Details

Only client test code and test data change.

- **Placement.** A new test argument, `placement`, names one operand:
`a`, `b`, `c`, `d`, `bias`, `scale_alpha_vec` or `workspace`.
`placement_offset` sets how many bytes of it lie below the boundary; 0,
the default, means half. `PlacedRegion` in the new `hip_placement.hpp`
uses HIP virtual memory management (`hipMemAddressReserve`,
`hipMemCreate`, `hipMemMap`). It reserves address space and maps the
operand so that it crosses a 4 GiB boundary.
- **Poison windows.** Two more mappings of the same size sit exactly 4
GiB below and above the operand. Both are filled with the fast_check
poison value, and so is the rest of the operand's own mapping past its
last element. A read whose carry was dropped returns poison, so
fast_check reports a wrong D instead of the GPU faulting. A write whose
carry was dropped, or that runs past the end, changes the poison, and a
device scan after every solution reports the element it was meant for.
- **The workspace.** Solutions need different amounts of workspace, and
any of them may use the whole size it is given. The placed workspace is
twice the largest size, and each solution starts it so that its own
reported range straddles the boundary with the whole workspace size
still mapped after it. A self-test checks that for sizes from 300 bytes
to the full workspace.
- **No silent fallback.** A placement case fails if no buffer was
placed, or if the placed range does not cross the boundary.
- **`HipDeviceBuffer`.** A new constructor allocates a buffer this way.
Every place that passes the operand to the library uses the placed
pointer without further changes. Its move operations now clear the
moved-from pointer. Before this, a moved-from buffer re-checked guard
bytes in memory owned by the moved-to buffer; that was harmless with
`hipMalloc`, but would fault on a placed buffer.
- **Requirements.** Placement requires `fast_check`. If the GPU or
platform lacks virtual memory management (Windows, for one), the test
skips with that reason. An invalid request fails the test.

## Test Plan

- `FastCheckDevice` self-tests:
- a kernel that computes addresses with the carry dropped on purpose
must read the poison value and have its write reported at the right
element;
- each solution's share of a placed workspace must straddle the boundary
and stay mapped, and a write past the end of a placed buffer must be
reported;
  - requests that cannot cross a boundary are rejected;
- a placed buffer keeps its address when `std::vector` growth moves it.
- pre_checkin cases: on a 1031 × 517 × 263 GEMM in f32 and bf16, place
A, B, C and D in turn, and bias and the scaleAlpha vector with their
epilogue, using the heuristic's first 16 solutions.
- nightly cases: the same placements, plus the workspace, with every
supported solution.
- `standard` tier, because the `HipDeviceBuffer` move change affects
every test.

## Test Result

**ROCm 10.2, at the stack tip (3364b793ac1, which includes this PR).**
Every fast_check case and self-test passed or skipped for a stated
reason on gfx90a (MI210), gfx942 (MI300X) and gfx950 (MI350X), with none
failing; the counts are in #12876. The placement cases all ran on all
three, including the workspace placement with every solution (more than
2,000 solutions on gfx90a, each with its own straddling range).

Earlier development runs:

- Self-tests (gfx942, MI300A): two deliberate mutations, one checking
only the window above and one never filling the poison, each made the
carry-drop self-test fail.
- The first version of the per-solution workspace placed each solution's
range near the end of a workspace-sized mapping, and solution 1 of the
every-solution case faulted the GPU: hipBLASLt may use the whole
workspace size it is given, not just the size its heuristic reports.
Doubling the mapping fixed it.
- `standard` tier (gfx942, MI300A, before the per-solution workspace
change): 71,379 run, 71,373 passed, 5 skipped, 1 failed,
`matmul_fallback_compute_fp16`, a workspace request larger than the test
allocates. At the stack tip under ROCm 10.2, all 16 of its cases pass on
an MI300X, so that failure looks tied to the MI300A or the ROCm 7.1
environment rather than to this PR.
- ROCM-32046 itself is on gfx1250 MXFP8 kernels, which none of these
machines can run.

